 # Reconstruction and Compositing Techniques
+
+
 ## Overview
-Reconstruction and Compositing Techniques encompass the mathematical and algorithmic methods used to transform discrete volumetric data into continuous signals and combine those signals into final pixel colors [G]. This family includes methods for evaluating polynomial fields along rays, resampling discrete data via convolution, and integrating optical properties using various compositing models [G]. It is distinguished from sibling methods by its focus on the underlying signal processing (reconstruction) and the specific mathematical formulation of the accumulation (compositing), ranging from standard alpha blending to differentiable training passes and importance-driven modulation [G].
+Reconstruction and Compositing Techniques encompass the mathematical and algorithmic methods used to map discrete volume data to continuous image samples and combine those samples into a final pixel color. This family solves the problem of accurately representing continuous volumetric fields from discrete grids while managing the computational cost of ray integration. It is distinguished from its siblings by its focus on the specific mathematical formulations of sample reconstruction (e.g., convolution, feed-backward gathering) and the integration of optical properties (e.g., polynomial composition, importance-driven modulation) rather than just the standard iterative accumulation loop.
 
 ## Shared principles
-All compositing methods in this family operate on a sequence of samples $(c_i, \alpha_i)$ encountered along a ray, where $c_i$ is the sample color (RGB) and $\alpha_i$ is the sample opacity (A) [S1, unofficial][S2]. The core mechanism involves an accumulator state $(C_{acc}, \alpha_{acc})$ initialized to $(0, 0)$ (or background color/opacity) and updated iteratively for each sample [S1, unofficial][S2].
+All methods in this family operate on a sequence of samples $(c_i, \alpha_i)$ encountered along a ray, where $c_i$ is the sample color (RGB) and $\alpha_i$ is the sample opacity (A) [G]. The core mechanism involves an accumulator state $(C_{acc}, \alpha_{acc})$ initialized to $(0, 0)$ (or background color/opacity) and updated iteratively for each sample [G].
 
